  <h2 class="project-section-title">Challenges</h2>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Unknown multiple contacts:</strong> MPC-based methods for interaction tasks are valid based on a common assumption that the contact
      occurs at a single and known location, typically at the end-effector where a force/torque (F/T) sensor is installed. In an unstructured
      workspace, such as scenarios that involve physical interaction with humans or with unknown obstacles, unexpected contact may occur at multiple
      locations on the robot. The safe handling of multiple contacts has been demonstrated using tactile sensors; however, it is not straightforward
      how to handle unknown multiple contacts occurring outside the tactile sensor.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Real-time control without simplifying assumptions:</strong> simplifying assumptions on the robot model are common in the literature;
      e.g., some of the time derivatives are assumed to be constant over the prediction horizon, a quasi-static robot or a linearized robot model is
      employed, or the robot joints are assumed to be perfectly velocity or position controlled.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Key idea: explicit multi-contact feedback loop in the MPC</h2>
  <p>
    This work proposes a contact-feedback MPC framework that addresses complex interaction tasks in which multiple contacts may occur at arbitrary
    locations. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first attempt that embeds multiple unknown contacts in the MPC framework.
  </p>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      By making an explicit contact feedback loop using MCP-EP, force control can be accomplished even in scenarios with multiple contacts regardless
      of contact locations.
    </li>
    <li>
      By employing the Differential Dynamic Programming (DDP) algorithm, the proposed MPC operates in real-time for a 7-DOF manipulator without any
      simplifying assumptions on the robot model.
    </li>
  </ul>
