  <p>
    The multi-contact feedback is provided through <a href="{{ '/research/projects/mcpep/' | relative_url }}">MCP-EP</a>, which estimates multiple
    contact locations and forces using an F/T sensor mounted at the base and JTSs. The proposed MPC does not strictly rely on MCP-EP, while one can
    employ any method as long as multi-contact information is provided in real time. Since the contact-feedback MPC, MCP-EP, and the low-level torque
    controller (3 kHz) operate at different rates, each program is run with the latest available data.
  </p>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Technical components</h2>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">1. Spring contact model</h3>
  <div class="project-media-card">
    <a
      aria-label="Open full image: Spring contact model"
      href="{{ '/assets/img/research/multi-contact-mpc/spring_contact_model.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ><img
        alt="Spring contact model"
        decoding="async"
        loading="lazy"
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/multi-contact-mpc/spring_contact_model.webp' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      <strong>Left:</strong> ${}^{\{C_i\}}\mathbf{r}_{env,i}$ is computed within the contact frame $\{C_i\}$, whose origin coincides with
      $\tilde{\mathbf{r}}_{c,i}$ and whose z-axis aligns with $\tilde{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_i$. <strong>Middle:</strong> $\mathbf{r}_{env,i}$ and
      $\mathbf{K}_{env,i}$ are calculated by transforming coordinates from $\{C_i\}$ to $\{W\}$. <strong>Right:</strong> $\mathbf{r}_{c,i}$ is fixed
      on the robot surface, and the predictive contact force is derived from $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i = g(\mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}_i)$.
    </div>
  </div>
  <div aria-label="Equation 1" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
    $$ \boldsymbol{\lambda}_i = \mathbf{K}_{env,i}\,\Delta\mathbf{r}_i = \mathbf{K}_{env,i}\,(\mathbf{r}_{env,i} - \mathbf{r}_{c,i}), \qquad
    {}^{\{C_i\}}\mathbf{K}_{env,i} = \mathrm{diag}(0,\,0,\,k_{env,i}) $$
  </div>
  <p>
    Since only z-directional stiffness exists, any movement of the contact point within the xy-plane of the contact frame does not change the force.
    This implies that an unconstrained motion can be achieved in the tangential direction of the contact force. The contact model does not depend on
    the control input, which allows the MPC framework to incorporate contact force feedback by avoiding an algebraic loop between
    $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i$ and $\mathbf{u}$.
  </p>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">2. Optimal control problem and cost function design</h3>
  <p>The MPC iteratively solves a discretized optimal control problem (OCP) with the contact-involved robot dynamics:</p>
  <div aria-label="Equation 2" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
    $$ \mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})\ddot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{C}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}})\dot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{q}) =
    \boldsymbol{\tau}_c + \sum_{i=1}^{k} \mathbf{J}_i^{\top}(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{r}_{c,i})\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i $$
  </div>
  <p>
    The running and terminal costs consist of the costs for motion control and contact force control, where the cost for contact is designed to allow
    for defining different tasks on different links:
  </p>
  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Motion control:</strong> regulates the end-effector to the desired position and orientation, with a joint velocity penalization term.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contact force regulation:</strong> regulates the $i$th contact force to the desired value $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{i,des}$ in the
      direction determined by $\mathbf{A}_d$.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Force barrier:</strong> for safe handling of unexpected contacts, the allowed contact force is limited by a force barrier with a
      quadratic function, $c_\lambda(\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i\| - \lambda_{i,max})^2$, which is activated if $\|\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i\| &gt;
      b\,\lambda_{i,max}$ with $b \in (0, 1)$.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">3. Numerical solution of the OCP</h3>
  <p>
    The OCP is solved with the Differential Dynamic Programming (DDP) algorithm, specifically Box-FDDP, which allows box-constraints on the control
    input. The joint angle and velocity limits are handled by a penalization approach, and the partial derivatives of the cost functions and the
    contact-involved robot dynamics are derived analytically, e.g., $\partial\boldsymbol{\lambda}_i / \partial\mathbf{q} =
    -\mathbf{K}_{env,i}\mathbf{J}_i(\mathbf{q}, \mathbf{r}_{c,i})$. As a result, the MPC operates in real-time for a 7-DOF manipulator without any
    simplifying assumptions on the robot model. A planning horizon of 125 ms ($T = 5$, $\Delta t = 25$ ms) and $k_{env,i} = 3500$ N/m are used in all
    experiments.
  </p>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Results</h2>
  <p>
    The proposed multi-contact feedback MPC is validated in experiments on the DLR-KUKA LWR IV+ torque-controlled robot, with an ATI Omega F/T sensor
    mounted at the base. There is no pre-knowledge about the contacts; instead, the proposed MPC handles the unexpected contacts in real-time.
  </p>
